3)What does TF-IDF stand for? 
  Explain in plain language why the word "the" scores almost zero in TF-IDF, 
  while the word "photosynthesis" would score high.

    It is an acronym for ״Term Frequency–Inverse Document Frequency״.
    TF-IDF is a statistical method used to measure how important a word is to a document within a collection of documents (a corpus).
    ״Term Frequency (TF)״ measures how many times a word appears in a specific document.
    ״Inverse Document Frequency (IDF)״ measures how rare a word is across the collection of documents by considering in how many documents it appears.
    The TF-IDF score is calculated by multiplying the TF and IDF values. A high score means the word appears frequently in the current document but 
    rarely in other documents, making it important. A low score means the word appears in many documents, so it carries little useful information.
    For example, the word "the" appears in almost every document, so its IDF is very low and its TF-IDF score is close to zero. In contrast,
    the word "photosynthesis" appears in relatively few documents but is highly relevant when it does appear, 
    so it receives a much higher TF-IDF score.

