4)What is a sentence embedding? How is it fundamentally different from one-hot encoding? 
  Give one advantage embeddings have that one-hot vectors don't.

    A sentence embedding is a method used to represent a sentence as a vector based on its semantic meaning. 
    Sentences with similar meanings are represented by vectors that are close to each other in the embedding space, 
    while sentences with different meanings are represented by vectors that are farther apart.
    In contrast, one-hot encoding gives each unique word its own binary vector. 
    The length of the vector equals the size of the vocabulary, 
    and the representation does not capture any semantic meaning or relationship between words.
    One advantage of sentence embeddings is that they capture semantic similarity, 
    making it possible to perform semantic search, 
    find similar sentences, and cluster texts based on their meaning, 
    whereas one-hot vectors cannot.

