5)Explain cosine similarity in plain language. 
  If two document vectors point in almost the same direction, 
  what does that tell us about the documents they represent?

    Cosine similarity is a method used to measure how similar two embedding vectors are by comparing the angle between them.
    The smaller the angle between the vectors, the more similar they are.
    If two document vectors point in almost the same direction, 
    it means that the documents have very similar semantic meaning. 
    The closer the vectors point to the same direction, the more similar the documents are.

    Bonus: Euclidean distance can be a poor choice for comparing embeddings because it is 
            heavily affected by the magnitude (length) of the vectors. 
            Two embeddings may have similar semantic meaning but different magnitudes, 
            making their Euclidean distance large even though they are semantically similar.

