11)What is MCP (Model Context Protocol)? What problem does it solve for 
   AI coding assistants like GitHub Copilot? Name two examples of things 
   an MCP server might expose to an AI assistant.

    MCP (Model Context Protocol) is a protocol that allows LLMs to 
    connect to external tools and systems through a standard interface.
    Without MCP, an AI coding assistant such as GitHub Copilot would need a 
    different API or custom integration for every external tool, making AI systems difficult 
    to build, maintain, and scale. MCP solves this problem by providing one standard protocol that 
    allows the AI assistant to communicate with many different tools in the same way.
    For example, if you want to use GitHub Copilot in your project, without MCP you would need a 
    different integration for every tool your project uses. With MCP, the assistant can communicate with 
    many different tools through the same protocol, making the system much easier to build and scale.

    Examples of tools or resources an MCP server might expose include:

        File System – allowing the AI assistant to read, create, edit, and search project files.
        Terminal – allowing the AI assistant to execute shell commands such as running tests, installing packages, or building the project.
        Git Repository – allowing the AI assistant to inspect commits, branches, and create pull requests.
        Database – allowing the AI assistant to query or update information stored in a database.
        Documentation – allowing the AI assistant to access internal documentation or API references.
        Browser – allowing the AI assistant to browse websites or interact with web applications.

